AI safety's institutional collapse creates a governance vacuum
The last two weeks mark the most concentrated period of AI safety erosion in the industry's history.
Between January 30 and February 13, 2026, at least 15 senior AI staff departed major labs (OpenAI, Anthropic,
xAI), OpenAI dissolved its second safety team in two years and launched ads in ChatGPT, the U.S. government
formally stripped "safety" from its AI standards body, and Palantir cemented itself as the de facto government
AI partner—all while a 100-expert international report warned that voluntary AI governance is failing. For a
new AI governance startup like CSOAI/EI³, this convergence of safety exodus, regulatory vacuum, and surging
government demand represents a rare market-creation moment.
The resignation wave: 15+ departures in eight days
The week of February 6–13, 2026 saw an unprecedented cluster of senior AI departures spanning three major
labs, driven by distinct but converging pressures.
At OpenAI, three events landed within 48 hours. Zoë Hitzig, a research scientist and Harvard junior fellow,
resigned on February 10—the same day OpenAI began testing ads in ChatGPT—publishing a New York Times
op-ed titled "OpenAI Is Making the Mistakes Facebook Made. I Quit." She warned that ChatGPT holds "an
archive of human candor that has no precedent," including users' "medical fears, their relationship problems,
their beliefs about God and the afterlife," 
 and that advertising built on this data "creates
a potential for manipulating users in ways we don't have the tools to understand, let alone prevent."
 The same week, reporting revealed that Ryan Beiermeister, VP of Product Policy, had
been fired in early January after opposing OpenAI's planned "adult mode" for ChatGPT and raising concerns
about child exploitation safeguards. OpenAI cited sexual discrimination against a male colleague; Beiermeister
called the allegation "absolutely false." 
 Most consequentially, on February 11 OpenAI
disbanded its Mission Alignment team—the six-to-seven-person group created in September 2024 to ensure
AGI benefits humanity. Its leader, Josh Achiam, was reassigned to a new role as "chief futurist." 
This marks the second dedicated safety team OpenAI has dissolved; the Superalignment team was dismantled
in May 2024 after co-leads Ilya Sutskever and Jan Leike departed. 
At Anthropic, the departure carried unique symbolic weight. Mrinank Sharma, who led the Safeguards
Research Team since its inception in 2023, resigned on February 9 with a public letter 
 on X 
 that garnered nearly one million views. 
 Sharma—who holds a
D.Phil. from Oxford in statistical machine learning 
 and was affiliated with the Future of Humanity
Institute—wrote: "The world is in peril. And not just from AI, or bioweapons, but from a whole series of
interconnected crises unfolding in this very moment." 
 He directly critiqued Anthropic's internal
governance: "Throughout my time here, I've repeatedly seen how hard it is to truly let our values govern our
actions... where we constantly face pressures to set aside what matters most." 
 His final research
project documented thousands of incidents where AI assistants "distorted users' perception of reality on a daily
basis." 
 Anthropic clarified that Sharma was "not the head of safety nor was he
in charge of broader safeguards at the company," though multiple outlets had described him as such. 
 At
least three other Anthropic staff—Harsh Mehta, Behnam Neyshabur, and Dylan Scandinaro—also departed
around the same time. 
