At xAI, the exodus was the largest by headcount. At least nine public departures occurred between February
6–11, including two co-founders: Yuhuai (Tony) Wu (reasoning lead) and Jimmy Ba (research/safety lead,
who reported directly to Musk). Six of xAI's 12 original co-founders have now left. 
 Ba's farewell was
notably ominous: "Recursive self improvement loops likely go live in the next 12 months... 2026 is gonna be
insane and likely the busiest (and most consequential) year for the future of our species." 
 Musk
characterized the departures as a "reorganization" to speed growth; three departing staff announced plans to start
new ventures together. 
 The exits coincided with French authorities raiding X offices over Grok-
generated nonconsensual deepfakes of women and children, and the pending SpaceX acquisition of xAI
 in a ~$1.25 trillion all-stock deal. 
A critical pattern unites these departures: no evidence of formal coordination exists. As former OpenAI
researcher Daniel Kokotajlo previously stated, "It's not been like a coordinated thing. I think it's just people sort
of individually giving up." The timing cluster was driven by converging catalysts—OpenAI's ad rollout,
Anthropic's pursuit of a reported $350 billion valuation, 
 and xAI's corporate restructuring—but
the shared underlying concern is structural: commercial pressures are systematically overriding safety
commitments at every major frontier lab. 
