The governance landscape has fractured along political lines
The regulatory environment for AI has shifted dramatically in the past two weeks, with safety infrastructure
being actively dismantled at the federal level while state and international frameworks race to fill the gap.
The most consequential institutional change: the U.S. AI Safety Institute was renamed the Center for AI
Standards and Innovation (CAISI), with Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick explicitly removing the word
"safety" and declaring, "For far too long, censorship and regulations have been used under the guise of national
security. Innovators will no longer be limited by these standards." 
 CAISI's focus has narrowed to
national security risks and global competitiveness. 
 The AI Safety Institute
Consortium (200+ organizations) remains nominally operational but is widely expected to be wound down.
 Meanwhile, NIST's budget faces a proposed $325 million cut for FY2026, eliminating
approximately 500 positions. 
The Pentagon's AI posture has shifted equally dramatically. Secretary of War Pete Hegseth's January 9
memorandum created an "AI-first" warfighting force 
 with a $13.4 billion
FY2026 budget, 
 mandating "any lawful use" language in AI contracts and explicitly dropping
"responsible AI" and ethics language. 
 The GenAI.mil platform now offers Google Gemini and
xAI's Grok across defense networks, 
 including classified systems. The Trump administration's
December 2025 executive order established an AI Litigation Task Force to challenge state AI laws,
 with the Commerce Department required to identify "onerous" state
regulations 
 by March 11 
 and the FTC directed to 
 classify state-mandated bias
mitigation as a "per se deceptive trade practice." 
State governments are pushing back hard. New York's RAISE Act, signed 
